arXiv:2509.11548v1 [cs.CV] 15 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Weiming Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yan Shao
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jing Yang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yujing Lu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ling Zhong
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yuhan Wang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Manni Duan <span id="id1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\sthanks</span>Corresponding author
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Graphical user interface (GUI) grounding is a fundamental task for building GUI agents. However, general vision-language models (VLMs) struggle with this task due to a lack of specific optimization. We identify a key gap in this paper: while VLMs exhibit significant latent grounding potential, as demonstrated by their performance measured by Pointing Game, they underperform when tasked with outputting explicit coordinates. To address this discrepancy, and bypass the high data and annotation costs of current fine-tuning approaches, we propose three zero-shot auxiliary reasoning methods. By providing explicit spatial cues such as axes, grids and labeled intersections as part of the input image, these methods enable VLMs to articulate their implicit spatial understanding capabilities. We evaluate these methods on four GUI grounding benchmarks across seven open-source
and proprietary VLMs. The evaluation results demonstrate that the proposed methods substantially improve the performance of GUI grounding.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>
GUI Grounding, Vision-Language Model, Auxiliary Reasoning

</div><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span><sup id="id2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1</span></sup> Zhejiang Lab, Hangzhou, China 
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id2.2" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2</span></sup>China Mobile, Hangzhou Research and Development Center, China</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Graphical user interface (GUI) grounding is a specialized form of visual grounding that involves locating specific screen components based on natural language instructions. Beyond identification, this task requires the model to output precise screen coordinates, such as a click point or a bounding box. This capability is essential for GUI agents to translate user commands into adequate screen interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although vision-language models (VLMs) have achieved substantial progress in general multimodal understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, they often struggle with GUI grounding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Unlike natural images, GUI screenshots contain dense text, fine-grained icons and highly structured layouts. These features are not emphasized during standard VLM pretraining, resulting in poor grounding performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Using <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pointing Game</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, which considers grounding successful if the predicted point from the highest attention score falls within the ground truth region, we find that VLMs possess considerable latent grounding capabilities. However, when the same models are required to output explicit coordinates, their performance drops drastically. As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), the VLM fails to produce accurate coordinates through direct inference, which we refer to as Direct Prediction, whereas Pointing Game correctly identifies the target location via attention peak localization (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b)).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve better performance in GUI grounding, many methods have been proposed, but they often require large-scale annotated data. For instance, UGround uses 1.3 million screenshots with 10 million element references <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. AGUVIS relies on 4.2 million sample elements from screenshots <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Other models like CogAgent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, OS-ATLAS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and OmniParser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> depend heavily on extensive grounding data as well. Recently, some efforts have been made to reduce data dependence through fine-tuning with limited samples. Notable approaches include coordinate-free prediction in GUI-Actor <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, adaptive exploration policies in InfiGUI-G1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, and strategies for complex GUI environments in IRIS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, a class of methods is proposed to improve model performance by introducing structural or spatial cues to guide <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">auxiliary reasoning</span>, without modifying the model’s parameters. Previous work in general vision grounding includes Grid-Augmented Vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>,
which overlays a simple grid on natural images to provide explicit spatial references. Scaffold Prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> overlays a dot matrix with row and column indices as visual anchors, enhancing vision-language
coordination in VLMs. However, these approaches are neither specifically optimized nor validated in the GUI domain. More recent GUI-specific approaches, like attention-driven grounding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, iterative narrowing mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and data-efficient spatial reasoning frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, are effective, but typically require fundamental modifications to the reasoning process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Following the zero-shot auxiliary reasoning techniques, our work introduces three lightweight methods, namely <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Coordinate Scaffold</span>, <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Axis-Grid Scaffold</span> and <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mark-Grid Scaffold</span>, that enhance both open-source and proprietary VLMs with explicit spatial cues. The first two methods leverage visual overlays such as axes, grids and labeled intersections to preserve the model’s native reasoning process, while the third simplifies continuous coordinate prediction as discrete grid ID prediction. We evaluate these methods on four GUI grounding benchmarks with seven backbone VLMs. Our methods consistently improve click accuracy, defined as the proportion of test samples where the predicted location falls within the ground truth element’s bounding box <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, for the majority of the seven VLMs across all benchmarks. For instance, Mark-Grid Scaffold elevates the score of Gemini-2.5-Flash from a direct inference score of 5.50% to an exceptional 72.09% on ScreenSpot-v2. Our code is available at https://github.com/liweim/AuxiliaryReasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A significant performance gap between Direct Prediction and Pointing Game is discovered in open-source VLMs, demonstrating their latent GUI grounding capability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Three lightweight and zero-shot auxiliary reasoning methods are proposed to enhance VLM’s GUI grounding capabilities, with the distinct advantage of being compatible with both open-source and proprietary models.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A detailed investigation is conducted on seven open-source as well as proprietary VLMs on four GUI benchmarks, clarifying the unique strengths and application scenarios of each method.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11548v1/paper/fig1.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/238;" width="476" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">This figure compares different GUI grounding methods on a screenshot sample. (a) Direct Prediction shows a model’s attempt to directly output coordinates. (b) Pointing Game illustrates a model’s latent grounding ability by identifying the point of highest attention. (c) Coordinate Scaffold, (d) Axis-Grid, and (e) Mark-Grid Scaffold are the proposed auxiliary reasoning methods.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Unleashing the GUI Grounding Capability</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first detail the implementation of Pointing Game, a method we employ to investigate the latent grounding capability inherent in VLMs. Then, we propose three auxiliary reasoning methods that exploit this latent ability to enhance spatial reasoning and bridge the performance gap.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Implementation of Pointing Game</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our implementation of Pointing Game involves extracting and processing the attention maps from the model’s last text token to all image tokens, and then determining whether the corresponding point of highest attention lies within the ground-truth region. Formally, our procedure is defined as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the input prompt: “Where should I click if I want to <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{instruction}</span>?”. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times H\times N\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times H\times N\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> be the complete attention tensor, where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of layers, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of attention heads, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of tokens (including text, image and special tokens). Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I\subset\{1,\ldots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I\subset\{1,\ldots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of indices corresponding to the image tokens, and let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\ast}\in\{1,\ldots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\ast}\in\{1,\ldots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the last text token of the key phrase “<span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{instruction}</span>” from the input prompt. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="GT\in\{0,1\}^{H_{o}\times W_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GT\in\{0,1\}^{H_{o}\times W_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the binary ground-truth region, where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> are the height and width of the original image. The function <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:\mathbb{R}^{H_{i}\times W_{i}}\to\mathbb{R}^{H_{o}\times W_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:\mathbb{R}^{H_{i}\times W_{i}}\to\mathbb{R}^{H_{o}\times W_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a spatial resizing function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each layer <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\{1,2,\dots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\{1,2,\dots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the following steps are performed:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">First, the attention weights from the last text token to all image tokens are extracted and averaged as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{a}_{l}=\frac{1}{H}\sum_{h=1}^{H}A[l,h,t^{\ast},I]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>H</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{a}_{l}=\frac{1}{H}\sum_{h=1}^{H}A[l,h,t^{\ast},I]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A[l,h,t^{\ast},I]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A[l,h,t^{\ast},I]</annotation></semantics></math> represents the slice of the attention tensor for layer <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> and head <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, where the query token is the last text token at index <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> and the key tokens are all the image tokens at indices in the set <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Second, the averaged attention vector is reshaped into a spatial grid and then resized to match the original image dimensions:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{l}=R(\textit{Reshape}_{H_{i}\times W_{i}}(\bar{a}_{l}))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Reshape</mtext><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{l}=R(\textit{Reshape}_{H_{i}\times W_{i}}(\bar{a}_{l}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Third, the coordinate of the maximum value in the processed attention map is extracted:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{l},y_{l})=\arg\max_{(x,y)}M_{l}(x,y)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{l},y_{l})=\arg\max_{(x,y)}M_{l}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, it is determined whether the predicted coordinate falls within the ground-truth region:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{l}=\mathbb{1}\{GT(x_{l},y_{l})=1\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>G</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{l}=\mathbb{1}\{GT(x_{l},y_{l})=1\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟙</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function that equals 1 if the condition is satisfied and 0 otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The results across all layers are then combined using a union operation:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=\bigcup_{l=1}^{L}I_{l}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=\bigcup_{l=1}^{L}I_{l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that this implementation serves as a valuable diagnostic indicator of latent visual grounding capability, while it is not directly applicable for grounding inference because the optimal attention layer for grounding is task-specific and sample-specific. By exhaustively comparing the output of every layer against the ground truth, we report the highest observed accuracy as an indicator of the model’s potential GUI grounding capability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Auxiliary Reasoning Methods</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose three auxiliary reasoning methods to enhance the performance of VLMs in GUI grounding: Coordinate Scaffold, Axis-Grid Scaffold and Mark-Grid Scaffold. These methods provide additional spatial cues during inference, which improves the model’s ability to accurately locate and reason about objects within images.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Coordinate Scaffold</span> builds upon the existing Scaffold Prompting method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. While the original Scaffold Prompting method labels the anchor points with row and column indices, our modification uses the actual corresponding <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> coordinates of the anchor points within the image. This approach directly aligns with predicting explicit coordinates for GUI grounding. Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(c) illustrates an example of this method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Axis-Grid Scaffold</span> enhances a model’s spatial perception by adding a structured grid overlay. It places coordinate scales at 100-unit intervals on the image’s four edges and a corresponding grid across the image. This combination provides a detailed, explicit spatial structure that facilitates the model’s reasoning about object locations and relationships. An example is shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(d).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Marked-Grid Scaffold</span> simplifies continuous coordinate prediction as discrete grid ID prediction task. This is achieved by overlaying an <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\times 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times 8</annotation></semantics></math> grid on the input image, where each grid cell is labeled with a unique ID at its center. During inference, the model identifies the grid IDs corresponding to the object’s four extremities: leftmost, topmost, rightmost and bottommost. This initial bounding box is then used to crop and magnify the region of interest. The cropped region is proportionally resized to 512 pixels on its shorter side and the same <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\times 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\times 8</annotation></semantics></math> grid is applied. The model is then presented with both the original image annotated with the predicted bounding box and the magnified, grid-marked crop. The model again predicts the four grid IDs defining the object’s extremities within this finer-grained view. The final object coordinates are determined by calculating the center coordinates from these four grid IDs. An example of this process is shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(e).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section describes the experimental setup used to evaluate the performance of our proposed auxiliary reasoning methods.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1 </span>Benchmarks</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the methods on four GUI grounding benchmarks:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ScreenSpot</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>: A benchmark for GUI visual grounding, containing 1,272 single-step instructions with corresponding target elements. It covers a range of GUI platforms, including mobile, desktop and web environments.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ScreenSpot-v2</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>: A corrected version of ScreenSpot, which addresses annotation errors and ambiguous instructions while maintaining the same number of samples.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ScreenSpot-Pro</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>: A challenging benchmark for professional scenarios, featuring 1,581 expert-annotated tasks across 23 applications and three operating systems. It uses high-resolution screenshots with significant domain gaps compared to typical pretraining data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">UI-I2E-Bench</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>: It evaluates GUI grounding through natural language instructions aligned with diverse UI elements. This dataset comprises 1,477 instruction-element pairs and evaluates a model’s capability to locate diverse elements within complex contexts.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2 </span>Baselines</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the original Scaffold Prompting, two baselines are employed to compare with the proposed approaches:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Direct Prediction</span>: This baseline uses the original images without any modifications, providing a reference point for model performance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Grid-Augmented Vision</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>: This method overlays a <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\times 9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\times 9</annotation></semantics></math> black grid pattern onto the input images. The grid provides explicit spatial references to enhance the model’s spatial localization capabilities.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Results</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Pointing Game versus Direct Prediction</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this experiment, we compare Pointing Game with Direct Prediction across seven VLMs (Gemini-2.5-Flash <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, Claude-3.5-Sonnet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, Qwen2-VL-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, Gemma-3-12B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, Gemma-3-4B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and SigLip-400M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>) on four benchmarks. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2.1 Pointing Game versus Direct Prediction ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, there is a significant gap between the results derived from Pointing Game and Direct Prediction. Qwen2-VL-7B exhibits a smaller gap due to its specialized training for grounding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. In contrast, the Gemma-3 series demonstrates a larger gap because of the absence of grounding training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. A key finding is that the Gemma-3 series, incorporating SigLip-400M as its image encoder, attains a higher Pointing Game score after instruction tuning, indicating a significant improvement in text-image alignment.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11548v1/paper/fig2.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:405/337;" width="405" height="337" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A comparison of Direct Prediction and Pointing Game across four open-source VLMs on four GUI grounding benchmarks. The significant performance gap between Direct Prediction and Pointing Game motivates our work.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Auxiliary Reasoning Methods</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct a comprehensive comparison of the performance of three baseline methods and three proposed auxiliary reasoning methods across four GUI grounding benchmarks. The results are detailed in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.2.2 Auxiliary Reasoning Methods ‣ 3.2 Results ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ How Auxiliary Reasoning Unleashes GUI Grounding in VLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. In general, auxiliary reasoning methods outperform baselines for the majority of VLMs. Among them, Mark-Grid Scaffold yields the most substantial improvement. For instance, it elevates the performance of Gemini-2.5-Flash from a direct inference score of 5.50% to 72.09% on ScreenSpot-v2. In particular, we observe a performance decrease in Qwen2-VL-7B. We attribute this to its specialized grounding training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, which reduces the need for auxiliary modules. This suggests that our methods are more effective for VLMs without such task-specific optimization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison of GUI grounding methods across four benchmarks. All values are reported as click accuracy (%). Our proposed approaches are highlighted in light gray. The best results are indicated in bold, and the second-best are underlined. Entries marked with a dash (–) denote unavailable data. Note that Pointing Game is only applicable to open-source VLMs. As a CLIP-style model, SigLip does not generate natural language responses and is therefore evaluated exclusively using Pointing Game.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:258.6pt;vertical-align:-127.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-110.6pt,82.9pt) scale(0.609336085964717,0.609336085964717) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D8E4F7;">
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Proprietary</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="4"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Open-Source</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_td"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> <span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gemini-</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.2.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2.5-Flash</span></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.2.2.2" class="ltx_text"></span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t"><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> <span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.5.1.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
